From: edde746 <86283021+edde746@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 03:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ao: decode the playing track to PCM when its passthrough
 output fails

ao_avfoundation's AVPlayer sink and ao_spatial fell back to PCM by latching
the failing format and reloading the output: the reopen refused the format and
the player decoded the track instead. ao_avfoundation's latch was
process-wide, and only its own PCM initialization cleared it. On macOS PCM
goes to coreaudio first and on tvOS to audiounit, so that never ran there: one
failure turned AC-3/E-AC-3 passthrough off for every later file and every mpv
instance in the process until it restarted. ao_spatial kept its latch per
player and cleared it the same way, which held only because it is the first
output tried.

The fallback also lost the audio the failed output had taken. AVPlayer pulls
up to twice the compressed lead before the output gives up, and the decoder
resumed after it: with video, audio stayed silent for about 14 s; audio-only
playback skipped that much; a file ending within that span stalled at its end
with no audio output open.

An output that cannot play its passthrough stream now reports
AO_EVENT_PASSTHROUGH_FAILED through ao_request_pcm_fallback(). The player
closes the output and, when that output fed the current passthrough chain,
switches the track's decoder to PCM with the chain's existing spdif_failed
state, then issues an exact refresh seek to the playback position. The next
track or file, or a later output reload, tries passthrough again. An event
from an output that outlived its chain (a gapless file change or a track
switch keeps it open) only closes it. Both outputs drop their latches, and
mpv_global loses ao_spatial's.

Checked on a MacBook Pro (macOS 27) with AirPods and a libmpv client whose
main thread never services the main queue, so AVPlayer never starts and every
E-AC-3 2.0 file falls back after 4.4 s. With video, audio resumes at 4.08 s in
sync (before: silent until 18 s); audio-only resumes at 0.07 s (before: 18.3
s); a 10 s file ends (before: stuck at 9.9 s). After the main thread is
serviced, the next file of the same player and a new player in the same
process play passthrough (before: "passthrough disabled after an earlier
player failure").

diff --git a/audio/out/ao.c b/audio/out/ao.c
index 0fd3c87..4fc4490 100644
--- a/audio/out/ao.c
+++ b/audio/out/ao.c
@@ -388,6 +388,13 @@ void ao_request_reload(struct ao *ao)
     ao_add_events(ao, AO_EVENT_RELOAD);
 }
 
+// Report that the AO cannot play its passthrough stream: the player core
+// destroys the AO and decodes the track to PCM. Fully thread-safe.
+void ao_request_pcm_fallback(struct ao *ao)
+{
+    ao_add_events(ao, AO_EVENT_PASSTHROUGH_FAILED);
+}
+
 // Notify the player that the device list changed. Fully thread-safe.
 void ao_hotplug_event(struct ao *ao)
 {
